## The reviewed code: five upstream projects

Each question inlines a diff (and surrounding file context) from a real merged
pull request. That content stays under its own project's licence, reproduced
here and repeated in every question's `source` block, with copyright notices
left intact.

| Project | PRs | Licence as declared upstream |
|---|---|---|
| Keycloak   | 10 | Apache-2.0 |
| Grafana    | 10 | AGPL-3.0 |
| Cal.com    | 10 | AGPL-3.0 (the `ee/` enterprise tree is separately licensed) |
| Discourse  | 10 | GPL-2.0 |
| Sentry     | 10 | FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0 |

Two notes on the last row, since it is the only non-OSI licence here. The FSL
permits redistribution provided the terms accompany the material and copyright
notices are not removed, and restricts only **Competing Use** — redistribution
as a commercial substitute for Sentry's own offering. A code-review benchmark is
not that. The licence also converts to Apache-2.0 two years after each release.

`build/fetch_prs.py` records the upstream repository, PR number and commit for
every case in the private manifest, so any question can be traced to its source.

**Not legal advice.** These are the licences as the upstream projects declare
them; if a specific file carries a different header, that header governs.
